---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Code from '../../../components/docs/Code.astro';
import DeviceConstraints from '../../../components/docs/DeviceConstraints.astro';
import { page } from '../../../data/docs-nav';

const entry = page('/docs/destinations/folder/');

const names = `hozz-20260822T211046Z-2f1a9c04-000000.ndjson
hozz-20260822T211046Z-2f1a9c04-000001.ndjson
hozz-20260822T221512Z-2f1a9c04-000002.ndjson`;
---

<DocsLayout page={entry} section="Destinations">
  <div class="fact-strip">
    <div>
      <dt>Format</dt>
      <dd>NDJSON, JSON, CSV or Metrics JSON</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Needs</dt>
      <dd>Nothing but the Files app</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Recommended</dt>
      <dd>Yes — start here</dd>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Hozz writes batch files into a folder you choose with the system file picker. Because iOS
    presents every provider through the same interface, that folder can be in iCloud Drive, Dropbox,
    OneDrive, Google Drive, an SMB share, or just on the device.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is the destination Hozz recommends first, and it is the one with the fewest ways to go
    wrong. It needs no server, no open ports and no VPN; the sync client already installed on your
    computer does the networking; and it keeps working while that computer is switched off — which a
    push to a local receiver cannot.
  </p>

  <h2>Setting it up</h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Choose a folder</h3>
      <p>
        Anywhere the Files app can reach. If you want it on a computer, choose a folder your cloud
        client already syncs there.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Whatever syncs that folder does the rest</h3>
      <p>Hozz writes files; your existing sync client moves them. Hozz has no part in that step.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Send a test</h3>
      <p>A probe file appears in the folder. If you can see it, the destination works.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What lands in the folder</h2>

  <p>Files are named so they sort chronologically and cannot collide:</p>

  <Code caption="hozz-&lt;timestamp&gt;-&lt;destination&gt;-&lt;sequence&gt;.&lt;ext&gt;" code={names} />

  <p>
    The destination identifier is part of the name because two destinations may point at the same
    folder, and every batch in one pass shares a timestamp and starts at sequence zero — without it
    their first files would be named identically and the second would silently replace the first.
  </p>

  <p>
    Pick the format on the destination:{' '}
    <a href="/docs/delivery-schema/#ndjson">NDJSON</a> (the default, and lossless),{' '}
    <a href="/docs/delivery-schema/#json">JSON</a>,{' '}
    <a href="/docs/delivery-schema/#csv">CSV</a>, or{' '}
    <a href="/docs/delivery-schema/#metrics-json">Metrics JSON</a>.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Line protocol is not offered for folders">
    <p>
      This is deliberate rather than an oversight. Hozz's own Mac app watches a folder for{' '}
      <code>ndjson</code>, <code>json</code> and <code>csv</code> and ignores anything else — so a folder
      writing InfluxDB line protocol would look like it was working while nothing was ever ingested.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Pairing it with the Mac app</h2>

  <p>
    A folder and the <a href="/docs/destinations/mac/">Mac receiver</a> are not alternatives. Point
    the phone at a synced folder, then point the Mac app's folder watcher at the same folder on the
    Mac, and you get the Mac app's charts and{' '}
    <a href="/docs/mcp/">assistant access</a> without either device needing to accept an inbound
    connection. On a network that blocks device-to-device traffic, this is the route that works.
  </p>

  <h2>When it goes wrong</h2>

  <p>Folders have exactly two failure modes, and both say so plainly:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><em>Hozz could not reach that folder. It may have been moved, renamed, or signed
          out.</em></strong>
      The saved reference no longer resolves — usually a renamed folder, or a cloud drive that
      signed out. Edit the destination and pick the folder again.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><em>Hozz no longer has permission to write to that folder.</em></strong>
      The folder resolves but iOS refused the write. Pick it again to re-grant access.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Neither loses data. A destination only advances its cursor past records it actually wrote, so
    once the folder is reachable again the same records are written.
  </p>

  <DeviceConstraints />
</DocsLayout>
